-- A `try` block over a `?` run, borrowing — the spelling plan 57 put in
-- `?.`'s place, and this pair is the **zero-cost claim moving with it**.
-- `optional_chain.sdx` was one of nine IR-equality pairs; deleting the
-- construct without pairing the replacement would have traded a measured
-- claim for an unmeasured one, which §6 declines.
--
-- Two things a pair here has to get right, and `optional_chain`'s first
-- attempt got both wrong. A chain is **field** access and not a method call,
-- so the pair needs a struct. And it yields `Option<&T>`, not `Option<T>` —
-- the reference is the lowering's contract, and `examples/fallible.sdx`
-- declares it the same way: `first_name : &Option<User> -> Option<&String>`.
--
-- The `&` is written now rather than inserted by a lowering, which §11.4 is
-- the finding for: a field access moves, so this is the one place the
-- deletion costs the author anything.

data Point = Point { x : i32, y : i32 }

borrowed_chain : &Option<Point> -> Option<&i32>
borrowed_chain p = try
  Some (&(.as_ref p)? .x)
